  "query_preview": "..(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 215 PM EST TUE NOV 17 2026\n\nAt the beginning of this period the high pressure regime that has \nbeen influencing our weather this week will be slowly moving off to \nthe east. Despite the high pressure moving out of the area it will \nstill be keeping us clear of any notable weather until later this \nweekend. Around this same time, a deep upper level trough will be in \nthe western US with an attached surface low and cold front slowly \nmaking its way to the east. Temps on Thursday should be mild to warm \nin the upper 60s to around 70. Areas in the highest elevations, like \nBlack Mountain, might be cooler around 60. Thursday night should \nfeature another night of ridge/valley splits with lows in the 30s to \nlower 40. High temps Friday through Sunday should be pretty similar \nto Thursday. Lows during this timeframe should also be similar, but \ngradually getting a bit warmer every night. \n\nOur next notable weather maker comes from the aforementioned upper \nlevel trough moving in from the west later this weekend. The cold \nfront and rain chances from this trough are progged to move through \nat some point Sunday. Models seem to be in good agreement of the \ntrough moving through, but aren't showing very much QPF at this \ntime. A vort max, 500mb height falls, and some DPVA with this trough \nall point towards enough synoptic lift to support PoPs at this time. \nAfter this front passes, high temps to open up next week should be \ncooler in the 50s or so. \n\nStarting around the early next week timeframe as well, there is a \nnotable trend that we will see a -AO/-NAO/+PNA pattern. It's \nuncertain how long the teleconnections will be aligned like this, \nbut the majority of model guidance has it lasting for at least next \nweek and probably to open up December. Confidence is highest in the \nPNA staying positive for the remainder of the month. Confidence is a \nbit lower in the AO/NAO staying negative for the rest of the month. \nEither way, this teleconnection combo should support a more active \nand meridional jet pattern with mixed in risks of colder than \naverage temps. Deterministic global models and ensembles do support \nthis deduction with solid agreement in waves and troughing across \nthe US next week. What exactly this all gives us here in eastern KY \nnext week and beyond is hard to determine, but a turn to a more \nactive pattern seems plausible at this point.",
